WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.760
A young sparrow never hears an adult sing.

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:09.660
As an adult it sings anyway, but its song is simple and abnormal.

3
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.280
It lacks the detail of a wild song.

4
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.600
The bird is not deaf, and its vocal organ works.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.680
Play it the right song later in life, and that does little to fix it.

6
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:21.180
What did it miss?

7
00:00:21.733 --> 00:00:23.853
It missed an experience at the right age.

8
00:00:24.313 --> 00:00:28.333
The nervous system expects certain input during a window of development.

9
00:00:29.073 --> 00:00:30.553
How sharp is that window?

10
00:00:31.193 --> 00:00:36.233
In this course, a window with sharp edges is a critical period, like a door.

11
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:39.753
A gradual one is a sensitive period, like a tide.

12
00:00:40.313 --> 00:00:42.493
Most of the windows we look at here are tides.

13
00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:44.867
Song learning has two phases.

14
00:00:45.447 --> 00:00:49.647
In the listening phase, a young bird hears a tutor and memorizes the song.

15
00:00:50.127 --> 00:00:54.467
In the practice phase, it makes rambling sounds and shapes them until they match the

16
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:58.987
memory. In white -crowned sparrows, the two phases can be months apart.

17
00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:02.467
A sparrow deafened between them sings a very poor song.

18
00:01:02.907 --> 00:01:04.787
It must hear itself to correct its voice.

19
00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:07.730
Language gives a human test.

20
00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:13.630
Johnson and Newport studied 46 adults who moved to the United States between ages 3

21
00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:17.870
and 39. Each judged whether English sentences were grammatical.

22
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.530
Those who arrived youngest scored like native speakers.

23
00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:25.590
Scores fell steadily with age of arrival up to about puberty.

24
00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:29.270
After that, scores were lower and varied widely.

25
00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.950
That does not look like a door shutting at puberty.

26
00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.310
What causes it is still debated.

27
00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:39.413
Babies cannot tell us what they see, so scientists watch where they look.

28
00:01:40.073 --> 00:01:42.233
Some children are born with dense cataracts.

29
00:01:42.813 --> 00:01:47.133
Light reaches the retina, but no pattern does, until the lens is removed.

30
00:01:47.853 --> 00:01:50.773
Daphne Maurer and colleagues followed 28 infants.

31
00:01:51.353 --> 00:01:55.693
Right after treatment, acuity was no better than a newborn's, whatever their age.

32
00:01:56.233 --> 00:01:59.813
Some improvement appeared after as little as an hour of visual input.

33
00:01:59.813 --> 00:02:04.253
So patterned input, not age alone, drives improvement.

34
00:02:04.993 --> 00:02:09.973
Yet some functions, such as fine acuity and the spacing of facial features, showed

35
00:02:09.973 --> 00:02:10.993
lasting deficits.

36
00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:14.147
Experience does more than fill the window.

37
00:02:14.787 --> 00:02:19.127
In zebra finches, the end of tutor learning is delayed in birds raised without a

38
00:02:19.127 --> 00:02:22.907
tutor. That suggests hearing a tutor helps close the window.

39
00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:25.427
Genes build the circuit that can learn.

40
00:02:26.207 --> 00:02:28.867
Genes and experience shape what is learned and when.

41
00:02:29.467 --> 00:02:33.327
Keep this rule. Most windows here are tides, not doors.
